Regression tests for type-checking performance.

Each file in this directory is known to be slow to type-check unless a specific performance
issue is fixed.  Each one runs a checker with `-AslowTypecheckingSeconds` set low enough that a
performance regression produces a "slow.typechecking" warning, plus `-Werror` so that the
warning fails the test rather than merely being logged.

Each test names its own checker in its `@compile` line, so tests for different checkers can live
side by side here.  Add a subdirectory per checker only if this directory becomes unwieldy;
`othervm.dirs` (see below) applies to subdirectories too.

`checker/jtreg/TEST.ROOT` lists this directory in `othervm.dirs`, so each file is compiled in a
fresh JVM.  Without that, a JVM already warmed up by earlier tests in the run reports quite
different times for the same file: Issue7023.java reported 14 seconds under `-samevm` and 11 in
its own JVM.

Each test file should document, in its `@summary`, which performance issue it guards against,
how long it takes to type-check when that issue is fixed, and how long it took before the fix.

Choosing a threshold:

 * `slow.typechecking` measures wall-clock time, so a file reports more when the machine is
   busy.  Measure on an otherwise idle machine, record the measurement in the file, and set the
   threshold to roughly twice it -- but well below the time the file takes without the fix.
 * Measure by running the test itself, not `javac` from the command line.  The two do not agree:
   Issue7023.java reports 5 seconds from the command line and 11 through jtreg.
 * The checker times nested scopes, and reports the innermost one that exceeds the threshold, so
   the number to compare against a threshold is the whole-class total -- roughly the sum of the
   members' times.  Lowering the threshold to 1 to "read off" the cost does not give it: an inner
   tree then trips first and suppresses the enclosing report.  Instead, set a candidate threshold
   and see whether the test passes.
